# Error Handling Rules

## Core Principle

Catch errors gracefully. Log the technical error internally, but return a generic, safe error message to the user.

## Error Handling Patterns

### Try-Catch Blocks

- Always handle errors appropriately
- Don't swallow errors silently
- Log errors with full context for debugging
- Return user-friendly error messages

### Error Types

- Use typed exceptions/errors
- Create custom error classes for different error types
- Distinguish between expected errors and unexpected errors

### Error Responses

- Log technical error details internally
- Return generic, safe error messages to users
- Don't expose internal system details
- Use appropriate HTTP status codes

## DO

- ✅ Catch errors and handle them appropriately
- ✅ Log full error details with stack traces for debugging
- ✅ Return user-friendly error messages
- ✅ Use appropriate error types and status codes
- ✅ Implement error boundaries (React) or error handlers

## DON'T

- ❌ Swallow errors silently
- ❌ Expose stack traces or internal errors to users
- ❌ Log sensitive information (secrets, PII) in error logs
- ❌ Use generic catch-all without proper handling

## Examples

### Good: Proper Error Handling

```typescript
async function getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
  try {
    const user = await db.users.findById(id);
    if (!user) {
      throw new NotFoundError('User not found');
    }
    return user;
  } catch (error) {
    logger.error('Failed to fetch user', { id, error });
    if (error instanceof NotFoundError) {
      throw error;
    }
    throw new InternalServerError('Failed to retrieve user');
  }
}
```

### Bad: Exposing Errors

```typescript
async function getUser(id: string) {
  const user = await db.users.findById(id); // ❌ No error handling
  return user; // ❌ May expose database errors to user
}
```